  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 211 AM CDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nThis morning, surface high pressure continues to expand southward \nover the area, bringing in a cooler and drier air mass, while the \nupper level ridge of high pressure has been shunted slightly westward\nover the High Plains. A shortwave is evident on water vapor imagery \nover western Nebraska where storms have formed. CAMs take that batch \nof rain eastward today, with a few models developing a showers\nsouthward over northern KS. Forecast soundings show a fairly dry \ncolumn below H7 and weak lift, so chances for precipitation will \nremain low today. More noteworthy is the cooler temperatures expected\ntoday. After about a week of highs near or in the 90s, highs will be\nback in the 80s area-wide this afternoon, with dew points in the 50s.\nThis is a welcome relief from previous sweltering heat and humidity.\n\nHigher POPs return to the forecast tonight. Showers and storms look \nto develop in central KS within a zone of isentropic ascent ahead of \nthe next mid-level shortwave trough, then track east across the area \ninto the early morning hours of Thursday. Forecast soundings shows \nbetter moisture availability, but limited instability and overall \nweak shear. Thus, the main risk looks to be isolated areas of heavy \nrainfall as the rain moves east. A wet and cloudy day will keep \ntemperatures even cooler, with some locations possibly staying the \n70s.\n\nAnother round of rainfall looks likely Thursday night and early \nFriday as a more notable shortwave rides through the northwest flow \naloft. Soundings show a very moist profile with tropical atmospheric \nmoisture from TS Bertha possibly making it this far north. Pwats \nover 2.0 inches rival daily max values from sounding climatology, and\nwith weak flow aloft, several hours of moderate rainfall could occur \nfor portions of the CWA. The axis for heaviest rainfall is currently \nfocused along and north of I-70 with the current QPF ranging from 1.0\nto 1.5 inches. However, combined with tonight's round of rain, some \nlocations could pick up over 2.0 inches total, which may lead to \nsome isolated flooding of area creeks and streams.\n\nOur brief period of wet weather ends this weekend when the upper \nridge expands over the region. Combined with stronger southerly low \nlevel flow, this sets the stage for more high heat and humidity. \nHeat index values look to exceed triple digits again by Saturday \nafternoon, continuing into early next week. Heat headlines may again\nbe needed for this timeframe.",
